Endocrine & Metabolic Disorders
BMI >30 kg/m2, Central adiposity distribution, Acanthosis nigricans, Exertional dyspnoea/joint pain

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-030
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, unmanageable bilateral knee pain and extreme shortness of breath when mobilizing within the home.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute deterioration in mobility, with the patient unable to walk to the bathroom due to excruciating joint pain and severe exertional dyspnoea. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was required to administer potent analgesia, rule out acute pulmonary embolism or acute coronary syndrome, and formulate a safe home mobility plan before standard clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My knees feel like they are grinding bone on bone and will collapse under me, and I can't catch my breath even just walking across the room."
History of Present Illness: Over the past month, the patient has experienced a progressive decline in mobility. Today, they report a severe, acute exacerbation of chronic joint pain, specifically in the weight-bearing knees and hips, rendering them essentially chair-bound. Any attempt to weight-bear causes agonizing pain and severe exertional dyspnoea, forcing them to sit immediately. They report profound generalized fatigue and lethargy. The partner confirms excessive daytime somnolence and loud snoring indicating severe obstructive sleep apnoea. The patient also complains of severe, burning heartburn (gastroesophageal reflux) when lying flat, preventing restful sleep.
Functional Status: Acutely chair-bound due to severe mechanical joint pain and respiratory compromise on exertion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme fall risk if attempting to mobilize unassisted. High risk of skin breakdown due to immobility.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/90 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 94 percent on room air | Wt: 145 Kg
General: Morbidly obese, distressed by joint pain, tachypneic after transferring from the chair to the examination bed.
Physical Exam: Physical examination reveals a markedly increased body weight and adiposity out of proportion to height, with a predominant central distribution of adipose tissue. Musculoskeletal exam demonstrates severe crepitus, reduced range of motion, and marked tenderness along the medial joint lines of bilateral knees, without acute effusions or erythema. Dermatological exam reveals intertrigo (erythematous maceration) in the deep abdominal skin folds, and prominent acanthosis nigricans on the posterior neck. Respiratory exam reveals decreased breath sounds globally due to body habitus, but no focal wheeze or crackles. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: BMI >30 kg/m2 (Calculated BMI 50.2 kg/m2), Central adiposity distribution, Acanthosis nigricans, and Exertional dyspnoea/joint pain.
Environmental Observations: Narrow hallways and low chairs present significant mechanical barriers and fall hazards for a patient of this habitus with acute joint failure.
Medication Reconciliation: Takes regular paracetamol and NSAIDs, but these are no longer effective for the mechanical pain. Regular omeprazole for reflux.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe End-Stage Osteoarthritis of the knees secondary to Class III Morbid Obesity - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The dyspnoea is strictly exertional and resolves quickly with rest, the heart rate is stable, and there is no sudden onset of pleuritic chest pain or hemoptysis.

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is bilateral, mechanical in nature, lacks a joint effusion, and the patient is afebrile.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The lack of crushing central chest pain, radiation, or diaphoresis, with the dyspnoea clearly linked to the massive mechanical effort required to mobilize the severe body weight.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute functional collapse driven by the massive mechanical strain of class III obesity on the weight-bearing joints, compounded by obesity hypoventilation. Triage accurately flagged the inability to mobilize and severe dyspnoea. Objective assessment confirmed severe, uncompensated mechanical joint failure without signs of acute infection or cardiopulmonary catastrophe. Delaying care until standard business hours forced the patient to endure severe pain, remain entirely immobile, and risk a severe traumatic fall. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was clinically mandated to provide potent bridging analgesia, assess cardiopulmonary stability, and establish a safe short-term mobility plan, fulfilling urgent MBS criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral tramadol 50mg to provide immediate, non-NSAID, centrally acting analgesia for the severe mechanical joint pain. Advised the application of topical antifungal/corticosteroid cream to the intertriginous areas to prevent secondary infection. Arranged urgent community physiotherapy and occupational therapy assessments.
Positive Behavioral Change: By utilizing a sturdy, bariatric-rated walking frame for all indoor transfers (action), you actively offload a significant percentage of your body weight from the failing knee joints to your upper body (cause), directly reducing the grinding pain and preventing a catastrophic fall (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral for community occupational therapy for home modifications (e.g., raised toilet seat, bariatric chair) and a bariatric walking frame.
DME/Safety (Optional): Prescription for a bariatric four-wheeled walker.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days to coordinate long-term weight management strategies (e.g., bariatric surgery referral, GLP-1 agonist therapy) and formal orthopaedic review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient experiences sudden, severe shortness of breath at rest, sharp chest pain, or coughing up blood, they must call 000 immediately, as their immobility and obesity place them at high risk for blood clots in the lungs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe pain and breathlessness are the direct physical result of the mechanical load on your joints and lungs. Definitive relief requires massive, sustained weight loss, likely through specialist intervention.
Clinical Course: The joint pain will continue to be a severe, limiting factor. Without significant weight reduction, the cartilage will completely erode, and mobility will be permanently lost.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk without the physical assistance of your partner until the heavy-duty walking frame is delivered. When resting, keep your legs elevated to reduce swelling.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The strong pain medication provided will temporarily dull the nerve signals from your knees, allowing you to make essential trips to the bathroom safely. However, it does not fix the underlying structural damage caused by the weight.
Actionable Steps: Use your arms to push up from chairs strictly. Wash and thoroughly dry the deep skin folds on your stomach twice daily to heal the rash. Schedule an appointment with your GP this week to discuss surgical or medical weight-loss options.

